Revision history for unreagent

{{$NEXT}}
  - hermes-setup merges into the existing Hermes config instead of replacing
    it: other keys and MCP servers survive, the original is copied to .bak and
    an existing .bak is never overwritten. If a release up to 0.0.9 wiped your
    config, the original is in config.yaml.bak.
  - hermes-setup edits only its own entry in the Hermes config: comments, key
    order, anchors and other MCP servers survive, and the managed marker sits
    on that entry. It refuses entries it cannot edit safely (anchored or
    aliased mcp_servers, anchors inside the old entry) and a dangling symlink
    at either file. Both files are written atomically, keep their permissions
    and follow symlinks; a new token is saved to unreagent.local.yaml only
    once the merge has succeeded.
  - hermes-setup refreshes only url and headers.Authorization in its entry and
    keeps the other keys, headers and comments you added there. It matches
    Authorization ignoring case, and refuses an entry that is a stdio server
    (command) or spells Authorization more than one way, or that it cannot
    edit in place (not a mapping, an alias, or brought in by a merge key).
  - hermes-setup refuses a Hermes config, or an unreagent.local.yaml it has to
    write, that holds more than one YAML document, instead of dropping all but
    the first.
  - hermes-setup symlink errors name the path: a loop no longer prints a bare
    "too many links", and a dangling chain names its missing end, read through
    symlinked directories.
  - hermes-setup no longer panics on an mcp.token shorter than eight
    characters, and only a token it generated is described as 64 hex chars.
  - run_command and the runtimes return as soon as the command exits, even
    when it leaves a process behind that holds its output (MSBuild node
    reuse); the job object is closed first, so the leftover tree dies with it.
  - Config placeholders now work in agent command/args/env, permission rules,
    hermes.configPath and the runtime executables. One that cannot be
    resolved is a load error wherever it is used; built-in compile/package
    are left out with a warning instead. Paths from a relative -config,
    UE_ROOT or engineRoot are made absolute first.
  - unreal.project must exist and takes no placeholders; mcp.writeConfig
    formats and negative delays are rejected at load time. Without an engine
    and with the default unreal.editor, the launcher (and hermes-setup) now
    stops at load with an error instead of starting a non-existent editor.
  - -files with an unresolved files.root is refused, and the file-tools
    startup line only appears when the MCP server is enabled.
  - The approve permission tool denies a request it cannot read (no tool
    name, or no tool input object) instead of handing it to the policy,
    where allow_all let it through with an empty input.
  - logs takes lines as a numeric string ("5") and answers a non-numeric
    value with an error instead of silently returning 50 lines.
  - The logs and run_command descriptions match the code: stop the editor
    before compile (Live Coding blocks UnrealBuildTool), command output
    never reaches logs, and logs service=agent stays empty while the agent
    runs in the foreground.
  - The ue_restart description now agrees with the compile advice (a restart
    also starts a stopped editor; stop it first to compile), and approve names
    a non-string tool name as such in its deny reason and logs <unknown> for a
    missing one.
  - The release archives (zip and tar.gz) include NOTICE with the third-party
    attribution.
  - The npm install of a node MCP bridge gives up after 5 minutes instead of
    blocking the launcher forever on a stalled registry; the bridge is left
    out with a warning and the editor starts (when a node_modules was already
    there it is kept, see below).
  - Ctrl-C during the bridge preparation stops it at once, and the editor is
    no longer started only to be killed again.
  - The bridge smoke test runs in a job object, so
    what the bridge spawned ends with it, and the launcher no longer waits
    on a leftover process that keeps the bridge's output open.
  - A service that fails 3 times in a row, each within 10 s of its start, is
    given up on instead of being restarted forever (a crash loop such as an
    agent that exits 1 at startup). maxRestarts stays 0 by default, meaning no
    fixed cap; a clean exit 0, a run longer than 10 s or a manual start or
    restart resets the count, and an explicit maxRestarts keeps its bound.
  - The launcher exits 1 when the agent fails and is not restarted, so scripted
    and CI runs see the failure; it also exits 1 with no agent and MCP off when
    the editor's last run never got going (failed to start, or failed within
    10 s). With MCP on it keeps serving and says so; a signal still exits 0.
  - start agent, r agent and r in the launcher console hand the terminal back
    to the agent, so the console and the agent no longer both read stdin.
  - ue_start and ue_restart fail, naming the cause, when the editor is not
    running afterwards. status shows the last start error as lastError until a
    start succeeds.
  - cleanOnRestart keeps Saved/Crashes on the automatic restart after a crash,
    so the dump can still be read. PackageRestoreData.json is still removed
    before every start, and the first and manual starts still empty
    Saved/Crashes.
  - Off Windows, the crash-reporter kill matches only the CrashReportClient and
    CrashReportClientEditor programs, no longer any process with the name in
    its command line.
  - In window mode the previous session's log is kept as unreagent.log.1; if
    it cannot be moved aside, the new session is appended to it. A log that
    cannot be created is reported on the console instead of being skipped
    silently.
  - File tools (read_file, list_dir, write_file, edit_file) can no longer
    leave files.root through a symlink or Windows junction, also when the
    target does not exist yet; a refused link names the path, the cause and
    the root. An absolute path is accepted only under the root; any other
    absolute or rooted path is refused as not relative. Windows reserved
    device names (CON, NUL, AUX, COM1, aux.h, ...) are rejected in any path
    component on every platform.
  - A bridge npm install that was cut short no longer makes the next start
    skip the install: once the bridge passes its smoke test, the launcher
    leaves a marker in node_modules, and a node_modules without it gets npm
    install again (the launcher deletes nothing). If that install fails, the
    node_modules already there is kept and the smoke test decides. When the
    smoke test fails over a node_modules from an earlier start, the log says
    how to repair it. A node_modules from before this version gets one npm
    install on the first start, and a bridge that keeps failing its smoke test
    gets npm install on every start.
  - A bare unreal.editor command is looked up on PATH: the demo's ping no
    longer warns "path not found" on every start, a name missing from PATH
    is reported as such, and one found only relative to the current
    directory, which exec refuses to start, is named as that.
  - read_file takes offset and limit (bytes): a negative offset counts from
    the end, so offset=-65536 returns the last 64 KiB of a log. A file not
    returned whole ends in a "[bytes X-Y of Z]" line with the total size and
    the offset to continue with, replacing the "[truncated]" marker. The file
    is no longer loaded whole, and range edges move to UTF-8 character
    boundaries.
  - New shipped skills ue-level-actors (spawn, move, delete and edit actors in
    the open level), ue-levels (open, create and save levels, survey what a
    level holds) and ue-assets (find, inspect, edit, save, move, delete and
    import Content Browser assets), written against the UE LLM Toolkit's tools.
  - New shipped skills ue-blueprints (read and change Blueprints with
    blueprint_query and blueprint_modify), ue-widgets (build and edit Widget
    Blueprints with widget_editor) and ue-enhanced-input (Input Actions,
    Input Mapping Contexts, triggers and modifiers with enhanced_input).
  - New shipped skills ue-python (execute_script and its script history),
    ue-tasks (poll, collect, list and cancel calls in the toolkit's task
    queue, and keep long calls clear of the 30 s timeout that may crash the
    editor) and ue-context (the toolkit's get_ue_context notes and its domain
    documents).
  - New shipped skills ue-viewport (capture_viewport: level, PIE and
    asset-editor previews, camera presets, animation frames, and how to get
    the JPEG into a file) and ue-pie-testing (gameplay_debug,
    run_console_command and get_output_log: scripted PIE runs with captures
    and a manifest, input injection, the input monitor, montages, console
    commands, and which log to read when).
  - New shipped skills ue-materials (create material instances, set their
    scalar, vector and texture parameters, and assign materials to skeletal
    mesh slots or level actors with material) and ue-niagara (inspect Niagara
    systems and their emitters, set user parameter defaults, and spawn a
    system as an editor preview with niagara).
  - New shipped skills ue-audio (inspect and list sound assets, create Sound
    Cues and attenuation assets, and add or edit audio components and ambient
    sounds with audio, including the undeclared target_path that
    set_audio_properties requires) and ue-metasounds (build and edit MetaSound
    Source graphs, set input defaults, add graph inputs and outputs, and
    preview them with metasound; needs UE 5.7 or a later 5.x).
  - The shipped UE skills now reach the agent through a new `skills` MCP tool,
    on by default. Its description lists every skill, and the `section`
    argument fetches a skill's sub-documents.
  - With `skills.materialize: true` (or the `-no-skills-mcp` flag, which also
    turns the MCP tool off) the skills are written as files into `skills.dir`
    on every start; the default is `${PROJECT_DIR}/.claude/skills`, where
    Claude Code finds them. Only `unreagent-*` directories there are replaced
    or removed, so your own skills stay, and skills dropped from a release are
    removed. A skill written under the `unreagent-` prefix gets its `name:` set
    to the directory name. If writing fails the launcher logs a WARN and
    starts anyway.
  - `skills.enabled: false` turns both paths off. Switching both paths off
    while `skills.enabled` is true is a load error.
  - An absolute unreal.editor path without .exe no longer logs "path not found"
    on Windows: the check resolves the path the way starting it does. A
    directory or a file without such an extension now warns there, since it
    does not start either.
  - Numeric tool arguments must be whole numbers within the int64 range:
    read_file offset=1.5 is refused instead of read as 1, and offset=1e20 is
    refused instead of silently reading the whole file (likewise logs
    lines=1e20, which returned the whole buffer).
  - New shipped skills ue-anim-blueprint (build and edit Animation Blueprints
    with anim_blueprint_modify: state machines, transitions and their
    conditions, anim graph nodes, bindings and layers) and ue-anim-montage
    (create and edit Animation Montages with montage_modify: sections and
    links, slot tracks, notifies, blend in and out, float curves).
  - New shipped skills ue-anim-sequence (edit AnimSequence assets with
    anim_edit: bone track keys, resampling, frame range extraction, additive
    settings, skeleton swaps; and fix skeletal mesh bones, bind pose and
    vertices) and ue-blend-space (create, inspect and edit 1D and 2D blend
    spaces and aim offsets with blend_space: axes, samples, saving).
  - A failed bridge smoke test now also logs the line that names the error,
    such as node's "Error: Cannot find module 'dep'", which a long stack trace
    used to push out of the last 15 lines of stderr shown.
  - agent.hermes.serverName is checked at load time while
    agent.hermes.enabled is set: it must start with an ASCII letter, hold
    only letters, digits, "_" and "-", and not be a word YAML reads as a
    boolean or null (yes, no, on, off, true, null, ...). Before, hermes-setup
    took any name, wrote some as a key that YAML reads as something else, and
    refused "<<" with an error that did not name the option. A bad name now
    stops every config load, a normal start included, not only hermes-setup.
  - run_command, run_python and run_node say when they cut the output: a
    line "[... N lines omitted]" leads the last 300 lines, and N also counts
    the lines dropped from the middle of an output past 2 MiB. That gap now
    reads "[... N bytes omitted]".
  - logs says when its 500-line buffer has dropped lines a request asks for:
    the reply starts with "[... N lines omitted; the log keeps only the last
    500]".
  - A taken MCP port (another unreagent or another program on mcp.address),
    or an mcp.address that is not a host:port of this machine, ends the
    launcher with exit 1 and an error naming the address, the cause and the
    fix, before it writes any file or starts the editor, the bridges or the
    agent. It used to be a log line while the launcher ran on and its agent
    talked to whatever held the port.
  - An MCP server that stops serving after the bind logs one ERROR line, in
    window mode also on stderr, saying that the agent's unreagent tools no
    longer work; everything else keeps running (the editor, and the agent if
    one is configured) so you can save, then restart unreagent. There is no
    automatic rebind.
  - "MCP server listening on ..." is logged only once the port is bound, so a
    client that connects on that line no longer gets "connection refused".
  - A .uproject EngineAssociation that names no usable engine (not installed,
    or installed without an editor binary) logs a WARN naming the association,
    the engine used instead and how to pin one (engineRoot or UE_ROOT), instead
    of falling back to the highest installed engine silently.

0.0.9     2026-11-13 19:32:24Z
  - ${ENGINE} is detected from the EngineAssociation in the .uproject:
    Launcher versions and source-build ids resolve, every registered Launcher
    install counts, and versions compare numerically (5.10 beats 5.9).
  - Permission layer: Bash rules match per shell segment (chains, grouping,
    substitution, nested shells); deny fires on any segment, allow only when
    every segment is covered. Deny sees through quoting and sudo/env/eval.
  - Non-Bash rules such as Read(...) or WebFetch(...) match the tool's path,
    pattern, url or query input, with normalised paths; a rule without an
    evaluable input fails closed.
  - The permission layer is enforced in headless mode only. On Windows the
    shell tool is PowerShell, so rules are written PowerShell(...).
  - MCP server hardening: foreign Origins get 403, POSTs need Content-Type
    application/json, bodies are size-limited, timeouts are set, and a
    panicking tool (even panic(nil)) becomes a tool error.
  - JSON-RPC handling follows the spec: "id": null is a request, batches are
    validated per element, bad framing answers -32600, initialize negotiates
    protocolVersion, and the Bearer scheme is case-insensitive.
  - write_file and edit_file reject a missing content or new_string argument
    (and edit_file an empty path) instead of truncating the file or deleting
    every match; read_file declares path required.
  - run_python and run_node run scripts inside the runtime's project
    directory, so imports resolve against the project; scripts left by a hard
    kill are swept at startup.
  - status lists a service with a stuck control loop as "unresponsive" instead
    of omitting it, and queries all services concurrently.
  - Process output is no longer lost: lines over 1 MiB are split instead of
    stalling the child, and a crashing process's last lines reach the log.
    run_command keeps only the head and tail of very large outputs.
  - A failed start (e.g. agent.command not on PATH) is reported as an exit
    instead of leaving a dead console; a failing Job Object setup is logged
    and the process runs unsupervised instead of crashing the launcher.
  - The taskbar flash feature (flash_task tool, "unreagent flash" sub-command,
    Stop hook) is removed; it never worked reliably.
  - Tool descriptions, log lines and error messages are English throughout.
  - make windows stops with a clear error when the .syso resource is an
    unfetched Git LFS pointer.
  - Building needs Go 1.26 or newer (the go directive was 1.19); CI and the
    release build use Go 1.27 and run the test suite before building.
  - README documents Unreal MCP (UE 5.8+, HTTP :8000) next to the UE LLM
    Toolkit, the refreshed requirements, MCP hardening, the Git LFS build
    note and a new architecture graphic; it links the unreagent-example
    project.
  - local.yaml overlays by YAML decode, not deep merge: a list replaces the
    base list, a map merges by key with each given entry replaced whole.

0.0.8     2026-06-12 05:42:04Z
  - Hermes Agent support: "unreagent hermes-setup" sub-command and the
    agent.hermes.* settings; see the README section on Hermes Agent.
  - Optional bearer authentication on the MCP server via mcp.token.

0.0.7     2026-06-12 03:15:56Z
  - Windows taskbar flash at the end of an agent turn: flash_task MCP tool and
    "unreagent flash" sub-command, with a Stop hook attached to Claude Code
    when agent.claudeIntegration is on.
  - Windows metadata: publisher and version in the .exe resource, asInvoker
    manifest, new icon; self-signed code-signing certificate with an import
    script.
  - Agent runs in its own window by default, inherits the real console in
    interactive mode, handles /quit without hanging the launcher and does not
    restart-loop on a missing program; agent.env sets extra variables.
  - MCP bridges (initialize handshake, in-editor plugin port) are checked
    before the agent starts; MCP config can be written as .mcp.json or for
    VS Code.
  - UE crash dialog and recovery prompt are handled unattended.
  - Flags -no-agent and -files; file tools read_file, write_file, list_dir
    and edit_file over MCP.
  - First release: launcher supervising the UE editor and an agent (Job
    Objects, restart policy, ring-buffer logs), MCP server with status, logs,
    ue_start/ue_stop/ue_restart, run_command, run_python, run_node and the
    approve permission tool, YAML config with ${ENGINE}/${PROJECT}
    placeholders and a local overlay, runnable ping demo.
